A1. These people are very close to each other. ~ SCOTCH
A6. Japanese head cheese. ~ MIKADO
A11. Lady Godiva's horse knew this was the right turn. ~ GEE
A12. A smart blow. ~ RAP
A14. Eggnog without the egg. ~ NOG
A15. This is a poetic island. ~ ISLE
A17. Drug store cowboy's dissipations. ~ SODAS
A19. The Spanish berry, or buck. ~ PESO
A21. Jewish quarters. ~ GHETTOS
A22. This autoist drives at a fine pace. ~ SPEEDER
A23. A Hebraic exclamation. ~ OY
A24. Stewed, oiled, pickled, or what were you. ~ TIPSY
A26. You and me. ~ WE
A27. A table scrap. ~ ORT
A29. This is born in France. ~ NEE
A30. A Denmark slipper. ~ SKI
A32. What most people who bought land in Florida are. ~ WISER
A34. This is an old salt. ~ EPSOM
A36. This heavenly old fellow runs a tannery. ~ SOL
A37. What Alphonse and Gaston tried to be. ~ POLITER
A38. That schoolgirl complexion. ~ TAN
A39. This is a bird. ~ PEWEE
A41. Fan again. ~ PEFAN
A44. A Scot will do this, before they bury him. ~ DEE
A45. A hiatus. ~ GAP
A47. This is hers in London. ~ ERS
A49. A river in Siberia. (If you care.) ~ OB
A51. A sheik's camping ground. (This requires a lot o' grit.) ~ DUNES
A53. Political "We." ~ ME
A54. They usually have good spirits at these parties. ~ SEANCES
A56. This makes everything on the level. ~ LEVELER
A58. Throat condition after a spirited party. ~ SERE
A59. Even if Tennessee is anti-evolution, it is still this. ~ STATE
A61. When a farmer is plucky, he can get this from a duck. ~ DOWN
A62. Domestic head cheese (init.). ~ MRS
A64. Same as Horizontal 23, only plural. ~ OYS
A65. Where the corn grows in achers. ~ TOE
A66. Pertaining to great American pill tossers. ~ PHYSIC
A67. This is the big noise in the orchestra. ~ CYMBAL

D1. A beer runner. ~ SPIGOT
D2. Eye language of the flirting flapper. ~ OGLE
D3. Seesaws. ~ TEETERS
D4. This is half o' cent. ~ CE
D5. Girls who used to do this knew their stuff. ~ PAD
D7. Preposition. (That's a lot of help.) ~ IN
D8. Russian coppers. ~ KOPECKS
D9. Good alibi for Dempsey's non-return. ~ AGED
D10. The kind of airs broadcasted from the stockyards. ~ ODORED
D12. When an acrobat feels himself slipping, he uses this. ~ ROSIN
D13. What the "So's your old man" remark is. ~ PASSE
D16. What the modern flapper is not. (Except as regards clothing.) ~ SHY
D17. A follower of the swallows. ~ SOT
D18. Peeping Tom found out that it didn't pay to do this. ~ SPY
D20. To use the needle. ~ SEW
D25. This bird always has a big bill to meat. ~ PELICAN
D27. A condition resulting from moonshine. ~ OILED
D28. Sitting Bull's bungalow. ~ TEPEE
D30. Vertical 27 got that way from being on this. ~ SPREE
D31. Greek "I's." ~ IOTAS
D32. Organist, fruit peddler, opera singer, etc. ~ WOP
D33. Unborn suckers. ~ ROE
D34. This is ever a contraction. ~ EEP
D35. Woman's detector. ~ MAN
D40. A dog chain. ~ WEINERS
D42. What most gentlemen married to blondes prefer. ~ FREEDOM
D43. This gets the dirt. ~ GOSSIP
D45. Appreciation with wim and wigor. ~ GUSTO
D46. A dealer in these, runs a skin game. ~ PELTS
D48. This is the whole thing in a nutshell. ~ KERNAL
D50. Manufacturer of sweets. (This is a stinger.) ~ BEE
D51. Dissipated Egotists Society (init.). ~ DES
D52. What Queen Marie said she came to America to do. (We shall see.) ~ SEE
D53. This is the kitten's. ~ MEW
D55. Men go to war in this. ~ ARMY
D57. Leopold's lifer long friend. ~ LOEB
D60. An affirmative in voting. ~ AYE
D63. So is this, in Spain. ~ SI
D65. A famous baseball Cobb. ~ TY


